To get the official firmware for your Tecno Spark 8 (KG6k) matching build KG6k-H6218MNOP-R-GL-230912V614, you will need to download it from trusted third-party firmware repositories. Tecno does not host a public, direct consumer download portal for their full stock ROM archives.
## Where to Safely Download the ROM
Search for your exact build string on reputable source platforms like:

* GSM Mafia
* Halabtech (requires a free account)
* FirmwareFile

## How to Extract the boot.img on Ubuntu
Because MediaTek firmwares are packed inside an archive, once downloaded, follow these steps to retrieve your boot image:
1. Unzip the main firmware file
If the downloaded archive is a .zip file, extract it using your Ubuntu terminal:

unzip Tecno_KG6k_Firmware_*.zip -d ~/tecno_firmware

2. Check the format inside the folder
Open the extracted directory (~/tecno_firmware) and check the file types:

* Scenario A (Loose files): If you see a file explicitly named boot.img, you are ready to copy it directly to your phone for Magisk patching.
* Scenario B (super.img or payload.bin): If your firmware contains a consolidated payload.bin file, you will need a extraction tool like payload-dumper-go to split it apart:

# Install the dumper tool via snap
sudo snap install payload-dumper-go
# Extract the boot partition out of the payload
payload-dumper-go -p boot ~/tecno_firmware/payload.bin


## Moving the file to your Phone for Magisk
Once you locate or extract the clean boot.img, send it to your phone's storage via ADB so you can open it inside the Magisk app to select "Select and Patch a File":

adb push ~/tecno_firmware/boot.img /sdcard/Download/

